From 5cffdad57ac29653d466b1a0f7b93bf39c7c52ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:27:29 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: cancel the timeout worker before dropping the
 IOMMU group

rocket_core_fini() puts core->iommu_group and clears the pointer, and only
then calls rocket_job_fini(). rocket_job_fini() is what stops the timeout
worker: drm_sched_fini() ends in
cancel_delayed_work_sync(&sched->work_tdr), and that delayed work runs on
core->reset.wq, which rocket_job_init() hands to drm_sched_init() as
timeout_wq.

The pointer is therefore NULL for the whole of drm_sched_fini() up to that
cancel, with the timer still armed across it. A job that times out in
there reaches rocket_job_timedout() and then rocket_reset(), which calls

	iommu_detach_group(NULL, core->iommu_group);

iommu_detach_group() takes group->mutex as its first act and does not test
the pointer, so this is a NULL dereference.

The worker does not have to start inside that window, which is what makes
the window wider than the two statements suggest. It only has to reach the
detach after the store. rocket_reset() calls drm_sched_stop() before it,
and drm_sched_stop() cancel_work_sync()s two work items and can then sit
in an uninterruptible dma_fence_wait() for a job that is still on the
hardware. A worker parked there when rocket_core_fini() runs is waited for
by the very cancel_delayed_work_sync() that was meant to have stopped it,
and takes the NULL on its way out.

Unbinding a core through sysfs with a job on the hardware is enough to
reach this, and only the 500 ms job timeout has to elapse.

Move rocket_job_fini() ahead of the put. The result is the reverse of the
order rocket_core_init() acquires things in: runtime PM was enabled last
so it is disabled first, then the job machinery, then the IOMMU group,
which rocket_core_init() takes before rocket_job_init() and which its own
error path already releases after it.

Runtime PM stays disabled ahead of the scheduler teardown, which is where
it belongs. Nothing rocket_job_fini() runs needs it: drm_sched_fini()
touches no NPU register, and a timeout worker still in flight only
decrements the usage count, with pm_runtime_put_noidle().
destroy_workqueue(core->reset.wq) stays where it is, after
drm_sched_fini(), because the scheduler's delayed work lives on that
workqueue.

The other worker rocket_job_fini() cancels, core->reset.work, has no
queueing site in tree, so the scheduler timeout is the only way to reach
the detach today.

Fixes: 0810d5ad88a1 ("accel/rocket: Add job submission IOCTL")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
index b3b2fa9ba..c41a61b4d 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
@@ -98,9 +98,9 @@ void rocket_core_fini(struct rocket_core *core)
 {
 	pm_runtime_dont_use_autosuspend(core->dev);
 	pm_runtime_disable(core->dev);
+	rocket_job_fini(core);
 	iommu_group_put(core->iommu_group);
 	core->iommu_group = NULL;
-	rocket_job_fini(core);
 }
 
 void rocket_core_reset(struct rocket_core *core)
-- 
2.43.0

